.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Speech9p
serves speech to every program at
.B /mnt/speech
(or
.IR mountpoint ):
what is said, heard as text, and text, said.
It holds no recogniser or synthesiser of its own: those are an
.IR engine ,
a directory of files described below, at
.B /mnt/speechengine
(or
.IR enginedir ).
Sound goes through the shared audio device
.RI ( audio (3)'s
mix): the microphone is a stream of its own at 16 kHz, opened only while
.B talk
is on, and what is said is a stream with the role
.BR speech ,
so a call lowers it and it lowers media.
.TP
.B talk
.B start
or
.BR stop :
push to talk.
While talk is on the microphone's sound goes to the engine; at
.B stop
the engine is told the utterance has ended.
A read gives
.B on
or
.BR off ,
followed by
.B asked
while an agent waits on
.BR ask ,
and
.B wake
while the microphone is open for the wake phrase;
each later read on the same open file waits until that changes, so a
program showing whether the microphone is open, or that something
wants an answer (Lucifer's indicator), reads it and need not poll.
Lucifer's is the microphone in its header
.RI ( dictate (1)).
.TP
.B listen
What is heard, a line a record, as the engine gives it:
.IP
.BI "partial confidence=" n " " words
.br
.BI "final confidence=" n " " words
.br
.BI "error " reason
.br
.BI "command " words
.IP
A partial is a guess while speech goes on; a final is what was said;
a command is what was said after the wake phrase (below).
The confidence is the engine's, from 0 to 1, and an engine that has none
gives none.
Every reader gets every record from its first read on: dictation, a
command reader and the dial pad can listen at once.
.TP
.B say
Text written is queued and spoken, a write an utterance.
A read gives
.BI "speaking " text
or
.BR idle .
.TP
.B cancel
Any write stops speech now: what is playing and what is queued.
.TP
.B chime
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.B done
or
.BR error :
a short sound, made here, as a stream with the role
.BR tone .
.B Speech9p
plays none of its own accord; whatever starts or stops talk says so if
it wants to.
.TP
.B voices
The engine's voices.
.TP
.B ctl
.BI "engine " dir\fR,
.BI "voice " v\fR,
.BI "lang " l\fR,
.BI "model " m
(the last three passed to the engine),
.B "duplex half"
or
.BR full ,
and
.B "agentmic off"
or
.BR on ,
and
.B "wake off"
or
.BI "wake " phrase\fR.
With
.B half
(the default) the microphone's sound is not given to the engine while
speech plays, so the system does not hear itself; use
.B full
only where the hardware cancels its own echo.
.B Agentmic
says whether an
.B ask
may open the microphone itself; it is
.B off
unless the user turns it on.
A read gives the settings, whether the engine answers
.RB ( "engine ok"
or
.BR missing ),
the settings file, the states of talk and speaking, and how many asks
wait.
The voice, language, model, duplex, agentmic and wake phrase are kept in the settings file
.RI ( settingsfile ,
or
.BI /usr/ user /lib/speech\fR),
as ctl lines, read when
.I speech9p
starts and written whenever one is set; they are given to the engine
before each utterance and each thing said, so an engine started later,
or again, has them.
.TP
.B ask
An agent's ear: write
.BI "start " ms
(30000 if no time is given, at most 120000) and read: the words of the
next utterance the user begins, then end of file; or end of file alone
if they begin none in time.
It does not turn talk on: the user answers by turning it on (a
microphone key, push to talk), and an utterance begun before the ask is
not given to it.
With
.B "agentmic on"
it turns talk on for its time, as
.B hear
does.
.TP
.B hear
The interface before this one, for its programs: write
.BI "start " ms
(5000 if no time is given) and read: the words of the next final, then
end of file.
Talk is turned on for that long, or until the final, unless it was on
already.
It is for a program the user has just asked to listen (a key pressed in
it), and is not an agent's.
.SS Lines
Speech is not only the room's.
A
.I speech9p
serves one audio path: this machine's, the shared device's mix, unless
.I audiodir
names another, a directory serving
.IR audio (3)'s
.B new
and its streams (a call's sound, a radio's).
Each path has a
.I speech9p
of its own, mounted where it is wanted
.RB ( /mnt/voice/ "line, say)"
with these same files, so what can talk and listen to the user can
talk and listen to whoever, or whatever agent, is on the line.
With
.B -o
the line is open: nobody there presses a key, so every utterance is
cut out at its silences (as for the wake phrase) and its final posted
to
.BR listen ,
and to an
.B ask
begun before it; talk reads
.B off
.BR line .
.B -o
and
.B -a
are given by whoever starts the line, and cannot be set through
.BR ctl .
Whether an agent may use a line is its name space's: a line is
granted, or not, as
.B /mnt/speech
is.
.PP
A
.I speech9p
posts only what is of its own utterances: an engine shared by two
lines gives its text to both, and each takes its own.
.SS The wake phrase
It is off unless the user sets it
.RB ( "wake hey veltro" ,
a phrase of one to four words); then the microphone opens only for
talk, at the user's word (a key, a tap).
Set, and while talk is off, the microphone stays open: what is said
is cut into utterances at its silences (a 20 ms stretch louder than
the quiet, a few of them to begin, 800 ms of quiet or 10 s to end) and
each is given to the engine.
Of each final only the words after the phrase go anywhere: as
.BI "command " words
to the readers of
.BR listen ;
a final without the phrase, and any partial, is dropped unread.
The phrase is heard a little loosely (a word a quarter of its letters
off), and one beginning
.BR hey ,
.BR ok
or
.B hi
is heard without it too
.RB ( Veltro
for
.BR "hey Veltro" ).
Said alone, a chime answers and the next utterance, within 6 s, is all
command.
With
.B "duplex half"
nothing is begun while speech plays.
Talk turned on closes it until talk is off again.
Every utterance goes to the engine, which on a machine elsewhere means
what is said in the room goes there; the emulator's own engine
.RI ( speech (3))
keeps it on the machine.
.SS Engines
An engine is a directory serving:
.TP
.B ctl
.BR start ,
.B end
and
.B cancel
bracket an utterance;
.BI "voice " v
and
.BI "lang " l\fR.
A read gives
.BI "name " n\fR,
.BI "rate " r
(the rate of
.BR tts 's
sound) and the engine's settings.
.TP
.B audio
The utterance's sound, written between
.B start
and
.BR end :
16 kHz, mono, 16-bit little-endian.
.TP
.B text
Records, as
.B listen
gives them; every reader from its first read on.
A final comes at
.BR end ;
partials, if the engine makes them, while the audio comes.
.TP
.B tts
Write text, then read its sound on the same open file to end of file:
mono, 16-bit little-endian, at
.BR ctl 's
rate.
.TP
.B voices
A voice a line.
.PP
An engine can be anything that serves these files: the one built into
the emulator
.RI ( speech (3),
.BR #W ),
a desktop's own programs
.RI ( speechcmd ,
below), or a machine elsewhere, its engine mounted over authenticated
9P, which is how a device without the computing for recognition (a
phone, a board) can use one its owner runs.
.SS Speechcmd
.I Speechcmd
is an engine of the host's programs, mounted at
.B /mnt/speechengine
(or
.IR mountpoint ).
Recognition is whisper.cpp's
.B whisper-cli
.RI ( sttcmd ,
with
.I sttmodel
its model) run on a WAV of the utterance written to the host's
.BR /tmp ;
it gives finals only.
Synthesis is, by host: macOS's
.BR say ,
.B espeak-ng
on Linux, or
.I ttscmd
(for Piper,
.BR "piper --model" " " \fImodel\fP,
whose raw sound is at
.IR ttsrate ,
22050 by default).
Text reaches the synthesiser on its standard input, never in a shell
line, and a voice or language is letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B -
and
.B _
only.
The programs are chosen by these options when it starts and cannot be
changed through
.BR ctl ,
which an agent may hold: whoever has
.B ctl
cannot choose what runs on the host.
.PP
The boot profile
.RB ( /lib/sh/profile )
starts both on a desktop, unless the emulator's own engine can hear,
when it is the engine instead.
.SH EXAMPLE
Say something, and listen to what is said:
.IP
.EX
echo 'Hello.' > /mnt/speech/say
cat /mnt/speech/listen &
echo start > /mnt/speech/talk
# ... speak ...
echo stop > /mnt/speech/talk
.EE
.PP
The old way, from a program that wants one answer:
.IP
.EX
{echo start 3000 >[1=0]; cat} <> /mnt/speech/hear
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /mnt/speechengine
.TP
.B /mnt/speech
.TP
.B /mnt/speechengine
.SS Agents
The Veltro tools
.B say
and
.B hear
are given /mnt/speech's
.B say
and
.BR voices ,
and
.BR ask ,
and nothing else of it: not
.B listen
(everything said, to anything),
.B talk
(the microphone's switch),
.B hear
(which turns it on) or
.B ctl
(the engine, and so where the microphone's sound goes).
A path grant of /mnt/speech gives nothing.
